 ### [SLAM and Mapping](9268-slam-and-mapping.md)
-This family encompasses methods that integrate Simultaneous Localization and Mapping (SLAM) with 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) or hybrid neural field representations to jointly estimate camera poses and reconstruct scene geometry/appearance. It solves the problem of real-time, high-fidelity 3D reconstruction and localization in dynamic or large-scale environments where traditional point-cloud or mesh-based SLAM struggles with textureless regions or complex lighting. It is distinguished from sibling methods like [Physical Parameter Optimization](4111-physical-parameter-optimization.md) by its focus on spatial-temporal consistency and pose estimation rather than material properties, and from [WildGaussians](6064-wildgaussians.md) by its explicit handling of camera trajectory optimization and loop closure.
+SLAM and Mapping in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) refers to the joint estimation of camera poses and the optimization of a 3D Gaussian scene representation from sequential or sparse sensor data. It solves the problem of building a consistent, high-fidelity, real-time renderable map in unknown environments, addressing challenges such as map drift, duplicate densification, and Gaussian degeneracy. It is distinguished from sibling methods like [Mapping Strategies](9321-mapping-strategies.md) by its focus on intra-scene consistency and pose estimation rather than cross-scene alignment, and from [Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) Representation](12096-neural-radiance-field-nerf-representation.md) by using explicit, differentiable Gaussian primitives instead of implicit neural fields, enabling real-time rendering and direct gradient-based optimization of geometry and appearance.
 
